// ora-mod-pim — generated by tools/merge.js: src/ora-mod-pim-*.js plus matching questions from the Oracle role banks (tools/modules.js).
QB['ora-mod-pim'] = (QB['ora-mod-pim'] || []).concat([["Product Hub","How would you architect product master data across PLM, Product Hub and downstream systems?","- **Engineering items/BOMs** authored in **PLM** (Oracle PLM/Innovation Management or third-party).\n- On release, items flow to **Product Hub** (master for enterprise item data) via change orders.\n- Product Hub enriches commercial, planning, costing attributes via **NIR** workflows and data stewards.\n- Publish to downstream: e-commerce, WMS, MES, CPQ via events/OIC.\n- **Global item numbering**, classes and data quality rules.\n- **Cross-references** to legacy and supplier/customer part numbers.","D"],["Product Hub","How would you architect item master governance with Redwood Product Management and AI?","- **Item classes, templates and rules** as the backbone.\n- **NIR/change order workflows** in Redwood with team-based steps.\n- **AI** for description generation, attribute enrichment from spec sheets, duplicate detection — proposals reviewed by stewards.\n- **Events** publish released items to WMS, e-commerce, MES.\n- Data quality KPIs on dashboards.","D"],["Product Hub","What are item classes and item templates?","- **Item class** (Fusion Product Hub) — a hierarchy that groups items (e.g. Raw Materials > Chemicals). Classes define **item numbering**, **attribute groups** (extensible flexfields), **templates**, **new item request** rules and data security. Child classes inherit from parents.\n- **Item template** — a set of default attribute values (e.g. \"Purchased Item\": purchasable, stockable, transactable, costing enabled). Applied when creating items to keep attributes consistent.\n\nEBS uses item templates and categories; Fusion adds item classes on top.","B"],["Product Hub","What is an item category and how is it used?","Categories group items for a business purpose. Each **catalog** (category set) serves one function:\n- **Purchasing catalog** — drives buyer assignment, approvals and spend reporting.\n- **Inventory catalog** — reporting and planning.\n- **Costing/GL catalog** — drives accounts via SLA rules.\n\nAn item can belong to several catalogs (one category per catalog unless multiple assignment is allowed). Many setups (e.g. approvals by category) depend on them, so design catalogs early.","B"],["Product Hub","What are item status codes and lifecycle phases?","- **Item status** (e.g. Active, Inactive, Prototype, Obsolete) controls key functional attributes — whether the item can be purchased, stocked, transacted, ordered or used in BOMs.\n- **Lifecycle phases** (Design, Prototype, Production, Obsolete) track where the item is in its life; changing phase can change status.\n- Changing status is how you stop an item from being ordered without deleting it.","D"],["Product Hub","How do you set up item data governance in Product Hub?","- Define **item classes** with required attributes and numbering.\n- Use **New Item Requests** with definition steps per function (engineering, procurement, planning, costing, quality) and an approval step.\n- **Validation rules** and **templates** for consistency.\n- **Change orders** for changes after release.\n- **Data stewards** per class; KPIs on cycle time and data completeness.\n- Integrate PLM (e.g. Oracle PLM or Agile) as the source for engineering items if applicable.\n\nResult: fewer duplicate items and complete attributes before transactions start.","D"],["Product Hub","How would you clean and migrate a legacy item master with many duplicates?","- **Profile** the data: duplicates by description, manufacturer part number, UOM inconsistencies, inactive items.\n- Agree **rules**: which items migrate (activity in last 24 months, open transactions, on-hand), golden record selection.\n- **Standardize** descriptions and UOMs; map to new item classes and categories.\n- Build **cross-reference** (legacy → new item) for open orders, BOMs and history.\n- Load via **item import** in stages; reconcile counts and key attributes.\n- Business data owners sign off each mock.","D"],["Product Hub","How would you design NIR workflows for a regulated company?","- Item classes with **required attributes** for regulated items (lot/shelf life, quality, storage).\n- **Definition steps** for engineering, quality, procurement, planning, costing.\n- **Approval steps** with electronic signatures where required.\n- **Change orders** for any later change; audit trail.\n- SLAs and dashboards for NIR cycle time.","D"],["Product Hub","What is a New Item Request (NIR) and how is it configured?","A **New Item Request** is a governed workflow for creating items.\n- Item classes are configured for **NIR**: which **entities** (attributes, structures, AML) are required and the **workflow steps** (definition, approval) with assignees.\n- Requester submits the NIR; **definition steps** route to teams to fill their attributes (e.g. costing, planning, purchasing).\n- **Approval steps** approve; once approved, the item is created/released.\n- Rules (validation rules) enforce data quality; **notifications** keep people informed.\n\nBenefit: clean item data and auditability instead of free-for-all item creation.","D"],["Product Hub","What are change orders in Product Hub?","Change orders control **changes to existing items or structures** (attributes, BOM components, revisions).\n- Created with affected items and proposed changes.\n- Route through **workflow** (review, approval), similar to NIRs.\n- On approval, changes are **implemented** (optionally with a new revision and effective date).\n- Keeps an audit trail of what changed, who approved and when — important in regulated industries (pharma, medical devices).","D"],["Product Hub","How do you troubleshoot an NIR stuck in approval?","- Open the NIR and check the **workflow step**: who is assigned, any errors.\n- Check the **approver/participant** setup on the item class workflow (inactive users, missing roles).\n- Check required attributes not completed in definition steps.\n- Reassign or update participants; for systemic issues, fix the item class workflow configuration.","D"],["Master Data","How would you lead an enterprise item master transformation?","- **Vision**: one item master (Product Hub), governed lifecycle from PLM to retirement.\n- **Assessment**: duplicates, attribute completeness, classification gaps.\n- **Data model**: item classes, attribute groups, structures, cross-references.\n- **Process**: NIR and change orders with data stewards; SLAs.\n- **Migration**: cleanse and consolidate; preserve history via cross-references.\n- **Adoption**: integrate all consumers (WMS, MES, e-commerce, CPQ) to Product Hub.\n- **KPIs**: duplicate rate, creation cycle time, completeness.","D"],["Product Hub","How would you use AI to clean a large legacy item master before migration?","- **Duplicate detection** with AI matching on descriptions, manufacturer part numbers, attributes.\n- **Classification** into new item classes.\n- **Attribute extraction** from descriptions and spec sheets.\n- **Steward review** of proposals with confidence scores.\n- Load clean data via import; track accuracy.","D"]]);
